<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>CAPACITOR_DEBUG</key>
	<string>$(CAPACITOR_DEBUG)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>Split Index</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<!-- Answers Apple's export-compliance question up front so App Store
	     Connect stops holding each build for a manual answer before it can go
	     to TestFlight or review. "false" means no NON-exempt encryption: the
	     app ships no cryptography of its own — no CryptoKit, no CommonCrypto,
	     no bundled algorithms — and everything it does encrypt (the WebView's
	     connection to splitindex.co.uk, plus Supabase, Stripe and RevenueCat)
	     is standard HTTPS/TLS provided by iOS, which Apple exempts.
	     Revisit this if the app ever encrypts anything itself, e.g. encrypting
	     cached sessions on disk with our own key rather than relying on iOS
	     file protection. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
    <false/>
    <key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
    <dict>
      <key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
      <array>
        <dict>
            <key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
            <string>Default Configuration</string>
            <key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
            <string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).SceneDelegate</string>
            <key>UISceneStoryboardFile</key>
            <string>Main</string>
        </dict>
      </array>
    </dict>
	</dict>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<!-- arm64, not armv7. armv7 is the 32-bit capability, dropped by iOS 11 —
	     Capacitor 8 targets far above that, so the old value described a device
	     this app cannot run on. -->
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Split Index uses your location to track distance and pace during a run.</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Split Index tracks your run in the background so tracking keeps going even with your phone locked.</string>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<!--
	  Two modes, both of which this app actually uses.

	  `location` for GPS recording with the screen locked, and
	  `bluetooth-central` for a chest strap or a Concept2 PM5 during that same
	  session. Without the BLE mode iOS silently stops delivering notifications
	  once the screen locks, and the run is saved with an average heart rate
	  that looks plausible and is wrong — the worst failure shape available.

	  Both are justified by functionality the app ships, which is what
	  Guideline 2.5.4 asks: it rejects a background mode declared without the
	  feature that requires it. NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription below names
	  the same hardware.

	  DELIBERATELY NOT HERE YET: `audio`.

	  SpeechPlugin.swift reads the per-kilometre split callouts aloud, and
	  those are silenced the moment the phone locks without that third mode —
	  which is the whole of the time an athlete is actually running. It is
	  still held back, on purpose, until 1.0 (5) is approved:

	    - STALE AS OF 22 SEP 2026, READ THE NEXT PARAGRAPH. The original
	      reason was that the callouts could not be heard during review
	      whatever this file said: the native shell loads production
	      (capacitor.config.ts), main is frozen for the review, and the JS
	      that calls the plugin was not on main. A background mode whose
	      feature is unreachable is exactly the shape Guideline 2.5.4 rejects.
	    - The app's largest structural risk is already Guideline 4.2 (a
	      reviewer's first screen is web content). Adding a third background
	      mode to the build that answers that is unpaid risk.

	  WHAT CHANGED. The speech branch was merged, so the calling JS IS on
	  main now: `src/lib/native/speech.ts`, called from
	  `src/app/(app)/cardio/gps-run/gps-run-client.tsx` on every completed
	  kilometre. Production serves main and the shell loads production, so the
	  feature is reachable by any athlete on the current build. The 2.5.4
	  argument above is therefore dead — `audio` is now JUSTIFIED rather than
	  unreachable, and its absence is what costs something.

	  THE LIVE TRADE, which is a decision and not an oversight. Shipping
	  without `audio` means the callouts are silent the moment the screen
	  locks — which, as the paragraph above says, is the whole of the time an
	  athlete is actually running. So the feature ships and fails in the only
	  case it exists for. Against that, the second bullet still stands: this
	  build carries the 4.2 risk, and a third background mode is one more
	  thing to explain. Decide it deliberately; do not inherit it from this
	  comment.

	  To add it: the single `<string>audio</string>` line below, bump the
	  build, and put the review-notes wording from the "After you submit"
	  phase of docs/pre-launch/submission-runbook.html alongside it. That
	  runbook still says to hold `audio` back and is stale for the same
	  reason this bullet was.
	-->
	<array>
		<string>location</string>
		<string>bluetooth-central</string>
	</array>
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Split Index connects to your heart rate monitor or rowing machine (Garmin, Polar, Concept2, or other standard Bluetooth devices) during a session.</string>
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Split Index reads your heart rate from Apple Health (including AirPods Pro) during a run.</string>
	<key>NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Split Index starts a workout session so AirPods Pro's heart-rate sensor turns on during a run — the workout itself isn't saved to Health.</string>
	<key>NSMotionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Split Index counts your steps during a run or walk to show your cadence.</string>
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>co.uk.splitindex.app</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
